{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And a woman, when she has a flow, the blood of her flow shall be in her flesh.</b> The meaning is the blood of her flow, and not a white discharge as in a man. <b>Seven days shall she be in her impurity (niddah):</b> even if she saw but a single sighting, seven days shall she be in her impurity." "<b>And behold,</b> this pericah speaks of the matter of the niddah, and the scriptural verses are closed (i.e., elliptical) concerning her — for there is no mention of immersion at all in it, except regarding one who touches her bedding, whom the verse required to immerse, as it says: 'he who touches her bedding shall wash his garments and bathe in water.' And it is found written in the responsum to Mar Yehudai Gaon: the immersion of the niddah is from the Torah by an a fortiori argument (kal va-ḥomer) from one who touches her bedding — and all the more so for one who touches her herself — and an a fortiori argument for the niddah herself, that she is in her impurity until she comes into the water. And thus our Sages of blessed memory received: 'seven days shall she be in her impurity (niddah)' — she shall be in her impurity until she comes into the water, for the Holy One, blessed be He, made it dependent on both days and immersion." "<b>And there are</b> among the Geonim those who derived this from the verse 'and they shall bathe in water' — this is a paradigm (av) for all the impure, that they remain in their impurity until they immerse. And our Sages of blessed memory said: all the labors that a woman performs for her husband when she is a niddah, she may perform for her husband, except for mixing the cup, making the bed, and washing his face, hands, and feet. Mixing the cup is the archetype of transgression; but if she changed her practice in it to make a recognizable distinction, it is permitted — as we say: the wife of Shmuel would change it with her left hand; and it is not to say that she passes it from her left hand to his hand, for that is forbidden even with any ordinary vessels, but rather she mixes and places it for him on the table with her left hand as a recognizable distinction. The wife of Abaye would place it at the mouth of the cup; the wife of Rava, behind the bolster. Making the bed in his presence is forbidden; not in his presence is permitted. Washing his face, hands, and feet — it is unnecessary to say in hot water, but even in cold water it is forbidden; and it is unnecessary to say if she touches and washes with her hands, for there is proximity of flesh and it is forbidden to touch her even with a small finger; but even if she pours and he washes, it is forbidden because the matter involves closeness of thought. And a person needs to distance himself greatly and to act stringently regarding the touch of a niddah; and thus our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Leviticus 18:6): 'לא תקרבו לגלות ערוה' — you shall not draw near to uncover nakedness — any closeness that leads to uncovering of nakedness. For since in a woman the vessel of formation lies, a person must conduct himself with her in purity so that the formation will be pure and fit to receive a pure soul; and it is well known that all the traits of the soul are drawn after the root of formation. And our Sages of blessed memory taught: one who lies with a niddah is liable to excision (karet); and even if she sleeps in her garments with her husband in one bed it is forbidden, as it is said (ibid.): 'ואל אשה בנדת טומאתה לא תקרב' — and to a woman in the impurity of her separation you shall not draw near — for the Holy One, blessed be He, warned Israel concerning purity and holiness, that they not do as the deeds of the nations." "<b>And if</b> you look into this pericah you will find that the Torah did not obligate a sacrifice for a niddah, but did obligate one for a zavah; and the reason is that the niddah-flow is an illness in the nature of a woman, and she is never cured of this illness, and therefore it makes her impure for seven days, whether she sees on one day or on all of them, for women by their nature do not have more than seven days, except when there is an excess of flow due to illness. But the zavah-flow is not an illness in her nature, and therefore the verse mentioned concerning her: 'ואשה כי יזוב זוב דמה ימים רבים בלא עת נדתה' — and a woman, when her flow continues many days beyond the time of her separation — that is, beyond the time known to her; or if she extends beyond the time with a flow of her blood for many days after the seven — behold, this is an illness like the flow of a man, and the verse required a sacrifice when she is healed, according to the law of the zav." "<b>Know that</b> this illness implanted in a woman — we do not find it among the females of other species, but in the human species alone, namely the woman. And this was by way of punishment from the sentence of Eve, for she was told (Genesis 3:16): 'הרבה ארבה' — 'I will greatly multiply,' etc. And therefore it is not found among other species, because animals have no intellect and their affairs do not follow punishment and reward. And it is possible to say that because the spirit of impurity had flown upon her from then ('מאז'), there is a power in the niddah woman to show more blood in appearance or in a glass after she gazes into them at the beginning of her niddah-period with a complete gaze." "<b>And you should further know</b> that even though it is an illness in her nature, yet it is a benefit and an advantage for the offspring, for the woman is cleansed of the superfluities and expels them, so that the offspring will be formed from the pure and choicest blood, in order that it be prepared to receive the divine influence, which is the light of the intellect — like the manner of iron, from which the rust is expelled, and then it is fit and prepared to receive the light of the sun, but not before. And for this reason it was fitting that this should occur in the human species, and not in the females of other species, whose breasts were placed to nurse milk in the place of the genitals; but not so in the human species, that is, the woman, whose breasts were placed in the place of the intellect, in order to arouse each person to what was the purpose of his creation."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:50.883159024Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}